  "prose": [
    {
      "text": "\"How does this traffic get to ZIA?\" splits into questions answered in order."
    },
    {
      "steps": [
        {
          "label": "Which methods exist, and which fits the site or user?",
          "text": "[[zia.traffic-forwarding.methods]]."
        },
        {
          "label": "Tunnels from a site.",
          "text": "GRE needs a static IP and has per-tunnel bandwidth limits: [[zia.traffic-forwarding.gre]], with health and failover in [[zia.traffic-forwarding.gre-failover]]. IPSec is the option for routers without GRE and for dynamic IPs: [[zia.traffic-forwarding.ipsec]], with its credential object in [[zia.traffic-forwarding.vpn-credentials]]."
        },
        {
          "label": "Browsers and existing proxies.",
          "text": "[[zia.traffic-forwarding.pac]] and [[zia.traffic-forwarding.proxy-chaining]]."
        },
        {
          "label": "Users who leave the network.",
          "text": "[[zia.traffic-forwarding.zcc]]."
        },
        {
          "label": "Several at once.",
          "text": "[[zia.traffic-forwarding.mixed-deployments]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "Once the traffic arrives.",
      "text": "How it learns the destination host (explicit versus transparent) is [[zia.proxy-mode]]; where ZIA sends traffic onward is [[zia.forwarding-control]]; how users are identified on each path is [[zia.authentication]]; which source networks are known is [[zia.locations]]; and how the firewall sees each path is [[zia.firewall.traffic-path]]."
    }
  ]
